In SAT, mass is an emergent property, but the closest proxy is what we call θ_4, the angle of the tangent to the helix that constitutes any given particle (you can see why that should roughly reproduce general relativity at larger scales I think) … worldline angle = timesheet motion ≈ mass … but it’s emergent because the interaction with the timesheet is not that simple… if a filament or bundle of filaments is perpendicular to the timesheet… That is, its θ_4 is a composite of the helical tangent and the gross orientation (ie rate of movement) … so the low angle of an individual coil means that intersect is moving around very quickly in a circle, and if the long axis of the helix is also tilted (=moving through space) then that’s also an increase in mass… Which becomes very noticeable at relativistic speed… for that matter, heat is the same thing, but due to small scale filament oscillations … same logic applies to larger filament bundles (nuclei, larger objects), except presumably the individual filament contributions to the composite angle are much lower and probably canceled out by one another…

SAT really is just an exercise and asking the question… What happens if you treat the four dimensional world line diagram of a particle as the reality. Everything else… And I mean everything else… follows directly from that

Now… I’m with you on a lot of that, but… I have a couple of different things to say here… Because SAT is strictly geometric… That means any nucleus with the same number of nucleons should behave roughly the same with respect to its geometric interaction with the time surface… That’s the idea of braid smoothing… A tight braid gives less active surface to interact with. So… Helium three and lithium three should behave more or less the same, no adjustment for expected mass… The Q count should count for it entirely. H2, and He2 should similarly have the same relationship to mass… H3 and He3, and Li3 should also scale to Q number. … in fact, SAT would probably predict, if we’re not fudging and we recognize this is probably a bit naïve… But from the District geometric point of view… Any isotope with the same number of nucleons should have the same mass, possibly with adjustment based on the presence and arrangement of electrons. I don’t know how SAT accounts for the difference between protons and neutrons precisely. And until we figure that out, SAT should technically predict that the proton and the neutron should have exactly the same mass, and any difference would probably be most consistently assumed to be a difference in the helical tangent of or direction of the quark coils in the proton and the neutron respectively,. Now I think that question is something we could probably put a pin in and address later. But hydrogen… that is an interesting microcosm… Because it should behave precisely like a proton… Which we think probably does have smoothing as we would expect all quark triplets to. But since it also has H-2 and H-3 isotopes, those have direct analogs to the nearby elements on the periodic table. So, H1>D2>T3 have mass scaling that looks a lot like H1>He2>Li3 (ignoring stability) …and the sequence n0>H2>He3 should scale roughly the same…  so we’ve got a little laboratory here to check our expectations. What do you think? Do we need to figure out the mass difference between protons and neutrons first?

﻿

, what we'll do is we'll carefully go though the SAT mass calculations for n0, p+, H1, H2, He2, H3, He3, Li3, Li4... and see what the Lagrangian has to say about them. While we're at it, we should look at the mesons. We'll go through this sector, see how far off we are and where and see if we can figure out why. If we can relatively easy, great; if not, we'll put a pin in it and move on.   

